/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · 1 Kings chapters 11-15
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("1kings", [
{n:11, name:"Solomon's Fall", seal:{ref:"1 Kings 11:11",verse:"Wherefore the LORD said unto Solomon, Forasmuch as this is done of thee, and thou hast not kept my covenant and my statutes, which I have commanded thee, I will surely rend the kingdom from thee, and will give it to thy servant."}, facts:["Did you know Solomon had seven hundred wives and three hundred concubines, and Scripture says plainly that his wives turned away his heart (1 Kings 11:3-4)?","Did you know the god Molech, for whom Solomon built a high place within sight of Jerusalem, was worshipped with child sacrifice (1 Kings 11:7)?","Did you know Jeroboam's mother is named in Scripture, Zeruah, a widow, a rare honor that marks the humble roots of Israel's coming king (1 Kings 11:26)?"], questions:[
{q:"From which nations did Solomon take his foreign wives?", options:["Moabites, Ammonites, Edomites, Zidonians, and Hittites","Philistines, Midianites, and Canaanites","Syrians, Elamites, and Persians","Greeks, Romans, and Egyptians"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:1",verse:"But king Solomon loved many strange women, together with the daughter of Pharaoh, women of the Moabites, Ammonites, Edomites, Zidonians, and Hittites;", insight:"Solomon did not fall at once; he gathered forbidden loves one nation at a time. Guard the small compromises, for they grow into a crowd."},
{q:"How many wives and concubines did Solomon have?", options:["Seven hundred wives and three hundred concubines","One hundred wives and fifty concubines","Twelve wives and no concubines","Three thousand wives in all"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:3",verse:"And he had seven hundred wives, princesses, and three hundred concubines: and his wives turned away his heart.", insight:"Seven hundred wives and three hundred concubines, and the text names the outcome plainly: his wives turned away his heart. No blessing protects a divided heart."},
{q:"What had the LORD warned about these nations?", options:["They will turn away your heart after their gods","They will bring famine upon the land","They will teach you their languages","They will steal your gold and silver"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:2",verse:"Of the nations concerning which the LORD said unto the children of Israel, Ye shall not go in to them, neither shall they come in unto you: for surely they will turn away your heart after their gods: Solomon clave unto these in love.", insight:"God had warned exactly what would happen, yet Solomon clave to them in love. Love for what God forbids is not romance; it is rebellion."},
{q:"When did Solomon's wives turn away his heart?", options:["When Solomon was old","In the first year of his reign","Right after the temple was finished","Before he married Pharaoh's daughter"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:4",verse:"For it came to pass, when Solomon was old, that his wives turned away his heart after other gods: and his heart was not perfect with the LORD his God, as was the heart of David his father.", insight:"It happened when he was old, after a lifetime of wisdom. A good beginning never finishes the race for you; stay tender to the end."},
{q:"Which two gods did Solomon go after?", options:["Ashtoreth of the Zidonians and Milcom of the Ammonites","Baal and Dagon","Molech and Rimmon","Chemosh and Nisroch"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:5",verse:"For Solomon went after Ashtoreth the goddess of the Zidonians, and after Milcom the abomination of the Ammonites.", insight:"He went after Ashtoreth and Milcom by name. Idolatry is always specific; name the thing that is stealing your worship."},
{q:"What did Solomon build in the hill before Jerusalem?", options:["A high place for Chemosh of Moab, and one for Molech","A new palace for Pharaoh's daughter","A temple for Ashtoreth","An altar unto the LORD"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:7",verse:"Then did Solomon build an high place for Chemosh, the abomination of Moab, in the hill that is before Jerusalem, and for Molech, the abomination of the children of Ammon.", insight:"He built high places for Chemosh and Molech in sight of Jerusalem. Sin tolerated near God's house soon stands inside it."},
{q:"Why was the LORD angry with Solomon?", options:["His heart was turned from the LORD, which had appeared unto him twice","He stopped building the temple","He refused to take counsel","He made war against Egypt"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:9",verse:"And the LORD was angry with Solomon, because his heart was turned from the LORD God of Israel, which had appeared unto him twice,", insight:"God had appeared to him twice, and still his heart turned. Privilege does not preserve; only obedience keeps the heart."},
{q:"What judgment did the LORD pronounce upon Solomon?", options:["He would rend the kingdom from him and give it to his servant","He would die without an heir","The temple would fall that very night","Jerusalem would be destroyed at once"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:11",verse:"Wherefore the LORD said unto Solomon, Forasmuch as this is done of thee, and thou hast not kept my covenant and my statutes, which I have commanded thee, I will surely rend the kingdom from thee, and will give it to thy servant.", insight:"The kingdom would be rent and given to his servant. What we refuse to surrender, God may take; hold everything with open hands."},
{q:"How much of the kingdom would Solomon's son keep?", options:["One tribe, for David's sake and for Jerusalem's sake","All twelve tribes","Nothing at all","The temple only"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:13",verse:"Howbeit I will not rend away all the kingdom; but will give one tribe to thy son for David my servant’s sake, and for Jerusalem’s sake which I have chosen.", insight:"One tribe would remain, for David's sake and Jerusalem's. Even in judgment, God keeps His promises to those He loves."},
{q:"Who was the first adversary the LORD stirred up against Solomon?", options:["Hadad the Edomite, of the king's seed in Edom","Rezon of Damascus","Shishak of Egypt","Hadadezer of Zobah"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:14",verse:"And the LORD stirred up an adversary unto Solomon, Hadad the Edomite: he was of the king’s seed in Edom.", insight:"God stirred up Hadad against Solomon. When we drift, God may let old enemies rise to get our attention."},
{q:"Where did Hadad flee as a little child?", options:["To Egypt","To Midian","To Tyre","To Babylon"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:17",verse:"That Hadad fled, he and certain Edomites of his father’s servants with him, to go into Egypt; Hadad being yet a little child.", insight:"Hadad fled as a little child to Egypt. The small survivors of past defeats can return as grown troubles."},
{q:"Whom did Pharaoh give Hadad to wife?", options:["The sister of Tahpenes the queen","His own daughter","A daughter of Solomon","A widow of Edom"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:19",verse:"And Hadad found great favour in the sight of Pharaoh, so that he gave him to wife the sister of his own wife, the sister of Tahpenes the queen.", insight:"Pharaoh gave Hadad his own wife's sister. The world honors those who oppose God's people; do not envy their favor."},
{q:"Who was the second adversary God stirred up?", options:["Rezon the son of Eliadah","Joab the captain of the host","Genubath of Egypt","Eliada the counselor"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:23",verse:"And God stirred him up another adversary, Rezon the son of Eliadah, which fled from his lord Hadadezer king of Zobah:", insight:"God stirred up another adversary, Rezon. One trouble unaddressed invites another; deal with the first drift quickly."},
{q:"Who lifted up his hand against Solomon from within Israel?", options:["Jeroboam the son of Nebat","Adoram who was over the tribute","Ahijah the Shilonite","Rezon of Damascus"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:26",verse:"And Jeroboam the son of Nebat, an Ephrathite of Zereda, Solomon’s servant, whose mother’s name was Zeruah, a widow woman, even he lifted up his hand against the king.", insight:"Jeroboam, Solomon's own servant, lifted his hand against him. Sometimes the threat rises inside the house, not outside it."},
{q:"What office did Solomon give the industrious Jeroboam?", options:["Ruler over all the charge of the house of Joseph","Captain of the host","Keeper of the treasury","Governor of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:28",verse:"And the man Jeroboam was a mighty man of valour: and Solomon seeing the young man that he was industrious, he made him ruler over all the charge of the house of Joseph.", insight:"Solomon promoted Jeroboam because he was industrious. God gives the diligent a place; faithfulness in small work leads to larger trust."},
{q:"What did the prophet Ahijah do with his new garment?", options:["Rent it in twelve pieces","Tore it in two","Gave it to Jeroboam","Burnt it before the altar"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:30",verse:"And Ahijah caught the new garment that was on him, and rent it in twelve pieces:", insight:"Ahijah rent his new garment in twelve pieces. God sometimes tears what is new to show what He is about to do."},
{q:"How many tribes would Jeroboam receive?", options:["Ten","Twelve","Two","Seven"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:31",verse:"And he said to Jeroboam, Take thee ten pieces: for thus saith the LORD, the God of Israel, Behold, I will rend the kingdom out of the hand of Solomon, and will give ten tribes to thee:", insight:"Ten pieces, ten tribes. God's word through Ahijah was precise; He keeps His promises down to the number."},
{q:"Why would Solomon himself not lose the kingdom in his own lifetime?", options:["For David his servant's sake","Because Solomon repented","Because Jeroboam had fled","Because the people loved him"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:34",verse:"Howbeit I will not take the whole kingdom out of his hand: but I will make him prince all the days of his life for David my servant’s sake, whom I chose, because he kept my commandments and my statutes:", insight:"Solomon kept his throne for David's sake. We stand in mercies earned by others; live worthy of the grace that covers you."},
{q:"Where did Jeroboam flee when Solomon sought to kill him?", options:["To Egypt, unto Shishak king of Egypt","To Damascus","To the wilderness of Paran","To Tyre"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:40",verse:"Solomon sought therefore to kill Jeroboam. And Jeroboam arose, and fled into Egypt, unto Shishak king of Egypt, and was in Egypt until the death of Solomon.", insight:"Jeroboam fled to Egypt until Solomon died. God's timing protects His purposes; wait in the shelter He provides."},
{q:"How long did Solomon reign in Jerusalem over all Israel?", options:["Forty years","Two and twenty years","Seventeen years","Threescore years"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 11:42",verse:"And the time that Solomon reigned in Jerusalem over all Israel was forty years.", insight:"Forty years of glory, ending in a divided legacy. Length of reign means little; faithfulness means everything."}
]},
{n:12, name:"The Kingdom Divided", seal:{ref:"1 Kings 12:24",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, Ye shall not go up, nor fight against your brethren the children of Israel: return every man to his house; for this thing is from me. They hearkened therefore to the word of the LORD, and returned to depart, according to the word of the LORD."}, facts:["Did you know Rehoboam was crowned at Shechem, the city where Joshua once renewed the covenant, and it was there that the kingdom broke apart (1 Kings 12:1)?","Did you know the 'scorpions' Rehoboam promised were likely barbed whips, a harsher bondage than anything Solomon had imposed (1 Kings 12:11)?","Did you know Jeroboam set his rival feast on the fifteenth day of the eighth month, exactly one month after the true Feast of Tabernacles in Judah (1 Kings 12:32-33)?"], questions:[
{q:"Where did all Israel come to make Rehoboam king?", options:["Shechem","Jerusalem","Bethel","Hebron"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:1",verse:"And Rehoboam went to Shechem: for all Israel were come to Shechem to make him king.", insight:"They crowned Rehoboam at Shechem, where the tribes had gathered for generations. New beginnings in old places still need new wisdom."},
{q:"Whom did Israel send for out of Egypt?", options:["Jeroboam the son of Nebat","Hadad the Edomite","Shishak the king","Rezon of Damascus"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:2",verse:"And it came to pass, when Jeroboam the son of Nebat, who was yet in Egypt, heard of it, (for he was fled from the presence of king Solomon, and Jeroboam dwelt in Egypt;)", insight:"Jeroboam came back from Egypt at the people's call. The exiled rival returns when the moment ripens; watch for God's timing in your troubles."},
{q:"What did the people ask of Rehoboam?", options:["Make the heavy yoke of thy father lighter","Give us land in Judah","Crown Jeroboam beside thee","Forgive all our debts"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:4",verse:"Thy father made our yoke grievous: now therefore make thou the grievous service of thy father, and his heavy yoke which he put upon us, lighter, and we will serve thee.", insight:"Their request was simple: lighten the yoke. People rarely rebel over nothing; listen before the grievance hardens."},
{q:"How many days did Rehoboam bid the people wait for his answer?", options:["Three days","Seven days","Forty days","One day"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:5",verse:"And he said unto them, Depart yet for three days, then come again to me. And the people departed.", insight:"He asked for three days to think. A pause before answering is wisdom; Rehoboam's failure came after the wait, not during it."},
{q:"What counsel did the old men give Rehoboam?", options:["Be a servant to the people, answer them, and speak good words to them","Tax them harder than thy father","Divide the kingdom thyself","Send Jeroboam back to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:7",verse:"And they spake unto him, saying, If thou wilt be a servant unto this people this day, and wilt serve them, and answer them, and speak good words to them, then they will be thy servants for ever.", insight:"The old men said: serve the people and speak kindly, and they will serve you forever. Leadership that serves is leadership that lasts."},
{q:"Whose counsel did Rehoboam forsake?", options:["The old men that stood before Solomon his father","The priests of Levi","The prophets of Bethel","The elders of Judah"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:8",verse:"But he forsook the counsel of the old men, which they had given him, and consulted with the young men that were grown up with him, and which stood before him:", insight:"He forsook the counsel of the old men. Whose voice you dismiss shapes the king you become; honor tested wisdom."},
{q:"What boast did the young men put in Rehoboam's mouth?", options:["My little finger shall be thicker than my father's loins","My sword is sharper than my father's","My house is greater than David's","My name shall be feared in Egypt"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:10",verse:"And the young men that were grown up with him spake unto him, saying, Thus shalt thou speak unto this people that spake unto thee, saying, Thy father made our yoke heavy, but make thou it lighter unto us; thus shalt thou say unto them, My little finger shall be thicker than my father’s loins.", insight:"My little finger thicker than my father's loins: cruelty dressed as strength. True strength never needs to boast about its weight."},
{q:"With what did Rehoboam threaten to chastise the people?", options:["With scorpions, where his father used whips","With swords instead of rods","With famine instead of tribute","With exile instead of labor"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:11",verse:"And now whereas my father did lade you with a heavy yoke, I will add to your yoke: my father hath chastised you with whips, but I will chastise you with scorpions.", insight:"Whips to scorpions, an escalation of pain. Threats may win a moment, but they lose a kingdom."},
{q:"How did the king answer the people?", options:["Roughly, forsaking the old men's counsel","Gently, with good words","He refused to answer at all","He wept before them"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:13",verse:"And the king answered the people roughly, and forsook the old men’s counsel that they gave him;", insight:"He answered roughly. A harsh answer stirs up anger; Rehoboam proved the proverb at the cost of ten tribes."},
{q:"Why did the king not hearken unto the people?", options:["The cause was from the LORD, to perform His word by Ahijah","He was afraid of Jeroboam","The old men stopped him","He had taken a solemn vow"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:15",verse:"Wherefore the king hearkened not unto the people; for the cause was from the LORD, that he might perform his saying, which the LORD spake by Ahijah the Shilonite unto Jeroboam the son of Nebat.", insight:"The cause was from the LORD. Behind Rehoboam's folly stood God's purpose; even human failure serves the divine plan."},
{q:"What was the people's cry of revolt?", options:["What portion have we in David? To your tents, O Israel","Long live the king","Give us a sign from heaven","We will serve thee forever"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:16",verse:"So when all Israel saw that the king hearkened not unto them, the people answered the king, saying, What portion have we in David? neither have we inheritance in the son of Jesse: to your tents, O Israel: now see to thine own house, David. So Israel departed unto their tents.", insight:"What portion have we in David? To your tents, O Israel. One speech can split what took generations to build; weigh your words."},
{q:"What happened to Adoram, who was over the tribute?", options:["All Israel stoned him, and Rehoboam fled to his chariot","He joined Jeroboam's service","He hid himself in the temple","He was made governor of Judah"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:18",verse:"Then king Rehoboam sent Adoram, who was over the tribute; and all Israel stoned him with stones, that he died. Therefore king Rehoboam made speed to get him up to his chariot, to flee to Jerusalem.", insight:"They stoned Adoram, and the king fled to his chariot. When leaders fail, their servants pay first; count the cost of pride."},
{q:"Whom did all Israel make king?", options:["Jeroboam; none followed the house of David but Judah only","Rehoboam over all Israel","Adoram the tribute master","Shemaiah the prophet"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:20",verse:"And it came to pass, when all Israel heard that Jeroboam was come again, that they sent and called him unto the congregation, and made him king over all Israel: there was none that followed the house of David, but the tribe of Judah only.", insight:"Only Judah stayed with David's house. Loyalty in the wreckage matters; God always keeps a remnant."},
{q:"How many warriors did Rehoboam gather to win back the kingdom?", options:["An hundred and fourscore thousand chosen men","Ten thousand","Three thousand","Fifty thousand"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:21",verse:"And when Rehoboam was come to Jerusalem, he assembled all the house of Judah, with the tribe of Benjamin, an hundred and fourscore thousand chosen men, which were warriors, to fight against the house of Israel, to bring the kingdom again to Rehoboam the son of Solomon.", insight:"One hundred and eighty thousand men gathered for civil war. How quickly brothers become armies; guard against the first division."},
{q:"What did Shemaiah the man of God command?", options:["Go not up, nor fight against your brethren; for this thing is from Me","Attack Jeroboam at dawn","Make a covenant with Jeroboam","Crown Adoram in his stead"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:24",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, Ye shall not go up, nor fight against your brethren the children of Israel: return every man to his house; for this thing is from me. They hearkened therefore to the word of the LORD, and returned to depart, according to the word of the LORD.", insight:"Ye shall not fight your brethren; this thing is from Me. God stopped a war with a word; obey even when your cause feels just."},
{q:"Which cities did Jeroboam build?", options:["Shechem in mount Ephraim, and Penuel","Bethel and Dan","Jerusalem and Hebron","Tirzah and Samaria"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:25",verse:"Then Jeroboam built Shechem in mount Ephraim, and dwelt therein; and went out from thence, and built Penuel.", insight:"Jeroboam built Shechem and Penuel. Men build to secure what God gave; trust the Giver more than the fortress."},
{q:"What did Jeroboam fear in his heart?", options:["If the people sacrificed at Jerusalem, they would kill him and return to Rehoboam","That Egypt would invade the land","That the prophets would curse him","That a famine would come"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:27",verse:"If this people go up to do sacrifice in the house of the LORD at Jerusalem, then shall the heart of this people turn again unto their lord, even unto Rehoboam king of Judah, and they shall kill me, and go again to Rehoboam king of Judah.", insight:"He feared losing his people to Jerusalem's altar. Insecurity invents idols; Jeroboam's fear built calves."},
{q:"Where did Jeroboam set the two calves of gold?", options:["The one in Bethel, and the other in Dan","Both in Shechem","In Jerusalem and Hebron","In Tirzah and Penuel"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:29",verse:"And he set the one in Bethel, and the other put he in Dan.", insight:"One calf in Bethel, one in Dan. Counterfeit worship is always convenient; true worship costs a journey."},
{q:"Whom did Jeroboam make priests of the high places?", options:["The lowest of the people, which were not of the sons of Levi","The sons of Aaron","The elders of Ephraim","Levites come up from Judah"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:31",verse:"And he made an house of high places, and made priests of the lowest of the people, which were not of the sons of Levi.", insight:"He made priests of the lowest of the people, not of Levi. When anyone can be a priest, no one truly is; honor God's order."},
{q:"When did Jeroboam ordain his counterfeit feast?", options:["The fifteenth day of the eighth month, devised of his own heart","The tenth day of the seventh month","The first day of the first month","The fourteenth day of the first month"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 12:33",verse:"So he offered upon the altar which he had made in Bethel the fifteenth day of the eighth month, even in the month which he had devised of his own heart; and ordained a feast unto the children of Israel: and he offered upon the altar, and burnt incense.", insight:"A feast devised of his own heart. Worship invented by man pleases man, never God."}
]},
{n:13, name:"The Man of God and the Lion", seal:{ref:"1 Kings 13:2",verse:"And he cried against the altar in the word of the LORD, and said, O altar, altar, thus saith the LORD; Behold, a child shall be born unto the house of David, Josiah by name; and upon thee shall he offer the priests of the high places that burn incense upon thee, and men’s bones shall be burnt upon thee."}, facts:["Did you know the man of God named Josiah about three hundred years before he was born, and Josiah later fulfilled the word by burning human bones on that very altar (1 Kings 13:2; 2 Kings 23:16)?","Did you know the lion killed the prophet yet ate neither the body nor the donkey, a restraint that marked the death as God's judgment rather than a wild attack (1 Kings 13:28)?","Did you know the old prophet's grave was spared when Josiah defiled the tombs at Bethel, because he had asked to lie beside the man of God (1 Kings 13:31; 2 Kings 23:18)?"], questions:[
{q:"Who came from Judah to Bethel by the word of the LORD?", options:["A man of God","Ahijah the Shilonite","Shemaiah the prophet","An angel of the LORD"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:1",verse:"And, behold, there came a man of God out of Judah by the word of the LORD unto Bethel: and Jeroboam stood by the altar to burn incense.", insight:"A man of God came from Judah by the word of the LORD. He is never named, yet his obedience is remembered; faithfulness outlives fame."},
{q:"What king did the man of God name, though not yet born?", options:["Josiah, a child to be born unto the house of David","Hezekiah","Jehu","Asa"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:2",verse:"And he cried against the altar in the word of the LORD, and said, O altar, altar, thus saith the LORD; Behold, a child shall be born unto the house of David, Josiah by name; and upon thee shall he offer the priests of the high places that burn incense upon thee, and men’s bones shall be burnt upon thee.", insight:"He named Josiah three centuries early. God's word reaches further than our sight; trust the long promise."},
{q:"What sign did he give the same day?", options:["The altar shall be rent, and the ashes poured out","Fire shall fall from heaven","The sun shall stand still","The river shall be dried up"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:3",verse:"And he gave a sign the same day, saying, This is the sign which the LORD hath spoken; Behold, the altar shall be rent, and the ashes that are upon it shall be poured out.", insight:"The sign came the same day: the altar rent, the ashes poured out. God confirms His word; watch for the sign He promised."},
{q:"What happened when Jeroboam stretched out his hand against the man of God?", options:["His hand dried up, so that he could not pull it in again","His hand was burned with fire","His arm was broken","His fingers were multiplied"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:4",verse:"And it came to pass, when king Jeroboam heard the saying of the man of God, which had cried against the altar in Bethel, that he put forth his hand from the altar, saying, Lay hold on him. And his hand, which he put forth against him, dried up, so that he could not pull it in again to him.", insight:"Jeroboam's hand dried up mid-gesture. The arm raised against God's messenger freezes; no one strikes God's servant with impunity."},
{q:"What did Jeroboam beg the man of God to do?", options:["Intreat the LORD and pray that his hand be restored","Curse the altar yet again","Stay and be his priest","Prophesy against Judah"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:6",verse:"And the king answered and said unto the man of God, Intreat now the face of the LORD thy God, and pray for me, that my hand may be restored me again. And the man of God besought the LORD, and the king’s hand was restored him again, and became as it was before.", insight:"The king who ordered his arrest now begged his prayers. Power bows when God moves; pray for those who oppose you."},
{q:"How did the man of God answer the king's offer of reward?", options:["If thou wilt give me half thine house, I will not go in with thee","He accepted the reward gladly","He asked for gold only","He demanded the crown itself"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:8",verse:"And the man of God said unto the king, If thou wilt give me half thine house, I will not go in with thee, neither will I eat bread nor drink water in this place:", insight:"Half thy house could not buy him. Some obedience has no price; decide beforehand what you will not sell."},
{q:"What threefold charge had the LORD laid upon him?", options:["Eat no bread, drink no water, nor return by the same way","Speak to no man, touch no altar, tarry three days","Take no gift, ride no ass, sleep not at all","Fast forty days, pray at Bethel, return to Judah"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:9",verse:"For so was it charged me by the word of the LORD, saying, Eat no bread, nor drink water, nor turn again by the same way that thou camest.", insight:"Eat no bread, drink no water, return by another way. God's instructions were exact; partial obedience would prove fatal."},
{q:"Who dwelt in Bethel and heard of all these works?", options:["An old prophet","Ahijah the Shilonite","A priest of the calves","Jeroboam's own son"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:11",verse:"Now there dwelt an old prophet in Bethel; and his sons came and told him all the works that the man of God had done that day in Bethel: the words which he had spoken unto the king, them they told also to their father.", insight:"An old prophet dwelt in Bethel, silent amid idolatry. Comfort near compromise dulls the conscience; he should have cried out long before."},
{q:"Where did the old prophet find the man of God?", options:["Sitting under an oak","Sleeping in a cave","Praying at the altar","Walking toward Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:14",verse:"And went after the man of God, and found him sitting under an oak: and he said unto him, Art thou the man of God that camest from Judah? And he said, I am.", insight:"Found sitting under an oak, resting after obedience. Rest is right, but rest is also when we are most easily turned aside."},
{q:"What lie did the old prophet tell?", options:["An angel spake unto me by the word of the LORD, saying, Bring him back","The king commands thy return","Josiah is already born","The LORD hath released thee from thy charge"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:18",verse:"He said unto him, I am a prophet also as thou art; and an angel spake unto me by the word of the LORD, saying, Bring him back with thee into thine house, that he may eat bread and drink water. But he lied unto him.", insight:"I am a prophet also, he said, but he lied unto him. Even a prophet's word must be tested against what God told you directly."},
{q:"What did the man of God then do?", options:["Went back with him, and ate bread and drank water","Fled in haste to Judah","Cursed the old prophet","Refused and departed"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:19",verse:"So he went back with him, and did eat bread in his house, and drank water.", insight:"He went back and ate. One meal undid a mission; small concessions carry the heaviest judgments."},
{q:"When did the word of the LORD come to the old prophet?", options:["As they sat at the table","In the night watches","At the altar in Bethel","Under the oak"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:20",verse:"And it came to pass, as they sat at the table, that the word of the LORD came unto the prophet that brought him back:", insight:"God's word came at the table, in the middle of the compromised meal. Judgment often arrives while we are still enjoying the disobedience."},
{q:"What sentence was pronounced upon the man of God?", options:["Thy carcase shall not come unto the sepulchre of thy fathers","Thou shalt die in Bethel this night","Thy sons shall perish with thee","Thou shalt never prophesy again"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:22",verse:"But camest back, and hast eaten bread and drunk water in the place, of the which the LORD did say to thee, Eat no bread, and drink no water; thy carcase shall not come unto the sepulchre of thy fathers.", insight:"Thy carcase shall not come to thy fathers' sepulchre. Disobedience forfeits honor; the saddest loss is a burial far from home."},
{q:"What met him by the way after he left?", options:["A lion met him and slew him","A band of robbers","A serpent in the path","Jeroboam's soldiers"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:24",verse:"And when he was gone, a lion met him by the way, and slew him: and his carcase was cast in the way, and the ass stood by it, the lion also stood by the carcase.", insight:"A lion met him and slew him. God means what He says; take His warnings as seriously as His promises."},
{q:"What strange sight did the old prophet find on the road?", options:["The ass and the lion standing by the carcase, which the lion had not eaten","The body vanished away","The lion sleeping upon the ass","The carcase turned to dust"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:28",verse:"And he went and found his carcase cast in the way, and the ass and the lion standing by the carcase: the lion had not eaten the carcase, nor torn the ass.", insight:"The lion stood by the body it would not eat. Even wild beasts obey God better than careless prophets; let creation shame our disobedience."},
{q:"How did the old prophet explain the death?", options:["It is the man of God, who was disobedient unto the word of the LORD","It was mere chance","The lion was sent by Jeroboam","He died of his long journey"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:26",verse:"And when the prophet that brought him back from the way heard thereof, he said, It is the man of God, who was disobedient unto the word of the LORD: therefore the LORD hath delivered him unto the lion, which hath torn him, and slain him, according to the word of the LORD, which he spake unto him.", insight:"He knew at once: the man of God was disobedient. The old prophet's grief was honest; those who lead others astray mourn the longest."},
{q:"What did they mourn over him, saying?", options:["Alas, my brother!","Alas, the prophet!","Woe unto Bethel!","Blessed be the LORD!"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:30",verse:"And he laid his carcase in his own grave; and they mourned over him, saying, Alas, my brother!", insight:"Alas, my brother! Judgment did not cancel love; weep over the fallen even when their fall was just."},
{q:"What did the old prophet ask of his sons?", options:["Lay my bones beside his bones","Bury me in Jerusalem","Burn my body with fire","Carry me back to Judah"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:31",verse:"And it came to pass, after he had buried him, that he spake to his sons, saying, When I am dead, then bury me in the sepulchre wherein the man of God is buried; lay my bones beside his bones:", insight:"Lay my bones beside his bones. He honored in death the man he misled in life; make what amends you still can."},
{q:"What did Jeroboam do after all this?", options:["He returned not from his evil way, but made priests of the lowest of the people","He destroyed the high places","He repented in sackcloth","He fled into Egypt"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:33",verse:"After this thing Jeroboam returned not from his evil way, but made again of the lowest of the people priests of the high places: whosoever would, he consecrated him, and he became one of the priests of the high places.", insight:"Jeroboam returned not from his evil way. Warnings without repentance only harden; do not admire the sign and ignore the message."},
{q:"What would become of the house of Jeroboam?", options:["It would be cut off and destroyed from off the face of the earth","It would reign over Israel forever","It would return to Judah","It would be carried to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 13:34",verse:"And this thing became sin unto the house of Jeroboam, even to cut it off, and to destroy it from off the face of the earth.", insight:"This became sin to cut off his house from the earth. A dynasty can be erased by one man's stubbornness; choose the better legacy."}
]},
{n:14, name:"Judgment on Jeroboam's House", seal:{ref:"1 Kings 14:16",verse:"And he shall give Israel up because of the sins of Jeroboam, who did sin, and who made Israel to sin."}, facts:["Did you know Ahijah was blind with age, yet the LORD told him the disguised woman was coming before she knocked, so his first words named her (1 Kings 14:4-6)?","Did you know the dying child Abijah was the only member of Jeroboam's house promised mourning and a grave, because God found some good in him (1 Kings 14:13)?","Did you know Shishak's invasion is carved on the temple walls at Karnak in Egypt, where his list of conquered towns matches the biblical account (1 Kings 14:25-26)?"], questions:[
{q:"Whose son fell sick at that time?", options:["Abijah the son of Jeroboam","Nadab the son of Jeroboam","Abijam the son of Rehoboam","Genubath the son of Hadad"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:1",verse:"At that time Abijah the son of Jeroboam fell sick.", insight:"Abijah the son of Jeroboam fell sick. Sickness in the palace drove the king to God; trouble is often mercy's messenger."},
{q:"What did Jeroboam tell his wife to do?", options:["Disguise herself and go to Shiloh to Ahijah the prophet","Send for the physicians","Offer sacrifice at Bethel","Flee with the child to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:2",verse:"And Jeroboam said to his wife, Arise, I pray thee, and disguise thyself, that thou be not known to be the wife of Jeroboam; and get thee to Shiloh: behold, there is Ahijah the prophet, which told me that I should be king over this people.", insight:"Disguise thyself, Jeroboam told his wife. He wanted God's word without God's gaze; you cannot hide from the One you seek."},
{q:"What was she to take to the prophet?", options:["Ten loaves, and cracknels, and a cruse of honey","Gold and silver","Two calves for sacrifice","Fine linen garments"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:3",verse:"And take with thee ten loaves, and cracknels, and a cruse of honey, and go to him: he shall tell thee what shall become of the child.", insight:"Ten loaves, cracknels, a cruse of honey. He sent peasant gifts to buy a prophet's word; God is not swayed by presents."},
{q:"Why could Ahijah not see?", options:["His eyes were set by reason of his age","He was blind from birth","A veil covered his face","The chamber was dark"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:4",verse:"And Jeroboam’s wife did so, and arose, and went to Shiloh, and came to the house of Ahijah. But Ahijah could not see; for his eyes were set by reason of his age.", insight:"His eyes were set by reason of age. Blind eyes did not blind the prophet; God sees for those who cannot."},
{q:"What did the LORD tell Ahijah beforehand?", options:["Jeroboam's wife would feign herself to be another woman","The child would surely live","Jeroboam would repent","She would bring no gift"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:5",verse:"And the LORD said unto Ahijah, Behold, the wife of Jeroboam cometh to ask a thing of thee for her son; for he is sick: thus and thus shalt thou say unto her: for it shall be, when she cometh in, that she shall feign herself to be another woman.", insight:"The LORD told Ahijah she would feign another woman. God warns His servants before the test arrives; listen before the knock."},
{q:"How did Ahijah greet her at the door?", options:["Come in, thou wife of Jeroboam; why feignest thou thyself to be another?","Who art thou, stranger?","Peace be unto thee","Take thy gift and go"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:6",verse:"And it was so, when Ahijah heard the sound of her feet as she came in at the door, that he said, Come in, thou wife of Jeroboam; why feignest thou thyself to be another? for I am sent to thee with heavy tidings.", insight:"Come in, thou wife of Jeroboam; why feignest thou thyself? Disguise is useless before God; come honestly or not at all."},
{q:"What charge did God lay against Jeroboam?", options:["He had done evil above all before him, and cast God behind his back","He had stolen the temple gold","He had murdered the priests","He had allied with Egypt"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:9",verse:"But hast done evil above all that were before thee: for thou hast gone and made thee other gods, and molten images, to provoke me to anger, and hast cast me behind thy back:", insight:"Thou hast cast Me behind thy back. That is the heart of idolatry: God demoted, carried like baggage, forgotten."},
{q:"What would God bring upon the house of Jeroboam?", options:["He would cut it off and take away the remnant as dung","He would make it great forever","He would send it to Babylon","He would forgive it wholly"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:10",verse:"Therefore, behold, I will bring evil upon the house of Jeroboam, and will cut off from Jeroboam him that pisseth against the wall, and him that is shut up and left in Israel, and will take away the remnant of the house of Jeroboam, as a man taketh away dung, till it be all gone.", insight:"He would take away the remnant as dung. God speaks bluntly about sin's end; take the warning before the sweeping."},
{q:"What would become of Jeroboam's dead?", options:["Dogs would eat them in the city, and fowls of the air in the field","They would lie in the kings' tombs","They would be buried in Judah","They would be burned with fire"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:11",verse:"Him that dieth of Jeroboam in the city shall the dogs eat; and him that dieth in the field shall the fowls of the air eat: for the LORD hath spoken it.", insight:"Dogs in the city, fowls in the field. No honorable burial for a dishonorable house; how we live writes our ending."},
{q:"When would the child die?", options:["When her feet entered into the city","At the midnight hour","In seven days","When the prophet prayed"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:12",verse:"Arise thou therefore, get thee to thine own house: and when thy feet enter into the city, the child shall die.", insight:"When thy feet enter the city, the child shall die. God's word keeps perfect time; the threshold became the tomb."},
{q:"Why would all Israel mourn the child?", options:["In him there was found some good thing toward the LORD","He was the eldest son","He would have been king","He was beloved of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:13",verse:"And all Israel shall mourn for him, and bury him: for he only of Jeroboam shall come to the grave, because in him there is found some good thing toward the LORD God of Israel in the house of Jeroboam.", insight:"In him some good thing was found toward the LORD. God noticed one child's heart in a corrupt house; He still notices yours."},
{q:"How would the LORD smite Israel?", options:["As a reed is shaken in the water","As chaff before the wind","As a vineyard trodden down","As a city without walls"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:15",verse:"For the LORD shall smite Israel, as a reed is shaken in the water, and he shall root up Israel out of this good land, which he gave to their fathers, and shall scatter them beyond the river, because they have made their groves, provoking the LORD to anger.", insight:"As a reed is shaken in the water. A nation without roots sways with every wind; be planted deep."},
{q:"Why would God give Israel up?", options:["Because of the sins of Jeroboam, who did sin, and who made Israel to sin","Because of Solomon's wives","Because of Rehoboam's pride","Because of the famine"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:16",verse:"And he shall give Israel up because of the sins of Jeroboam, who did sin, and who made Israel to sin.", insight:"Who did sin, and made Israel to sin. Leaders multiply their sin in followers; influence is a stewardship, not a toy."},
{q:"Where was Jeroboam's wife when the child died?", options:["At the threshold of the door in Tirzah","In Shiloh","At the gate of Bethel","In Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:17",verse:"And Jeroboam’s wife arose, and departed, and came to Tirzah: and when she came to the threshold of the door, the child died;", insight:"At the threshold the child died. The prophecy met her at the door; God's word never arrives late."},
{q:"How long did Jeroboam reign, and who followed him?", options:["Two and twenty years; Nadab his son reigned in his stead","Forty years; Abijah","Seventeen years; Baasha","Twenty and four years; Elah"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:20",verse:"And the days which Jeroboam reigned were two and twenty years: and he slept with his fathers, and Nadab his son reigned in his stead.", insight:"Two and twenty years, then Nadab. Long reigns end all the same; only what was done for God remains."},
{q:"How old was Rehoboam when he began to reign, and how long did he reign?", options:["Forty and one years old; he reigned seventeen years","Twenty years old; forty years","Thirty years old; three years","Sixty years old; one year"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:21",verse:"And Rehoboam the son of Solomon reigned in Judah. Rehoboam was forty and one years old when he began to reign, and he reigned seventeen years in Jerusalem, the city which the LORD did choose out of all the tribes of Israel, to put his name there. And his mother’s name was Naamah an Ammonitess.", insight:"Forty and one, and seventeen years in Jerusalem. Numbers mark a reign; character marks a man."},
{q:"What was Rehoboam's mother's name?", options:["Naamah an Ammonitess","Tahpenes the queen","Maachah of Abishalom","Zeruah the widow"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:21",verse:"And Rehoboam the son of Solomon reigned in Judah. Rehoboam was forty and one years old when he began to reign, and he reigned seventeen years in Jerusalem, the city which the LORD did choose out of all the tribes of Israel, to put his name there. And his mother’s name was Naamah an Ammonitess.", insight:"His mother was Naamah an Ammonitess. Solomon's foreign marriages bore fruit in his own son's throne; sowing reaches further than we think."},
{q:"What did Judah build in those days?", options:["High places, and images, and groves, on every high hill and under every green tree","A new temple","Walls around Jerusalem","Altars unto the LORD only"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:23",verse:"For they also built them high places, and images, and groves, on every high hill, and under every green tree.", insight:"High places on every hill, under every green tree. Idolatry loves beautiful places; worship the Creator, not the scenery."},
{q:"In which year of Rehoboam did Shishak come up against Jerusalem?", options:["In the fifth year","In the first year","In the tenth year","In the seventeenth year"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:25",verse:"And it came to pass in the fifth year of king Rehoboam, that Shishak king of Egypt came up against Jerusalem:", insight:"In the fifth year, Shishak came up. Judgment has a date on God's calendar, even when men forget."},
{q:"What did Shishak take from Jerusalem?", options:["The treasures of the house of the LORD and of the king's house, and the shields of gold","Only the cattle of the field","The ark of the covenant","Nothing; he was turned back"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 14:26",verse:"And he took away the treasures of the house of the LORD, and the treasures of the king’s house; he even took away all: and he took away all the shields of gold which Solomon had made.", insight:"He took the shields of gold Solomon had made. Glory borrowed from God can be carried off in a day; hold it loosely."}
]},
{n:15, name:"Asa the Reformer", seal:{ref:"1 Kings 15:11",verse:"And Asa did that which was right in the eyes of the LORD, as did David his father."}, facts:["Did you know Asa removed his own mother Maachah from being queen for making an idol in a grove, then burned the idol by the brook Kidron (1 Kings 15:13)?","Did you know Asa raided the temple treasury to bribe Ben-hadad of Syria, spending dedicated silver and gold on a political alliance (1 Kings 15:18)?","Did you know Baasha's slaughter of Jeroboam's house fulfilled Ahijah's prophecy word for word: he 'left not to Jeroboam any that breathed' (1 Kings 15:29)?"], questions:[
{q:"In which year of Jeroboam did Abijam begin to reign over Judah?", options:["The eighteenth year","The twentieth year","The second year","The fifth year"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:1",verse:"Now in the eighteenth year of king Jeroboam the son of Nebat reigned Abijam over Judah.", insight:"Abijam began in Jeroboam's eighteenth year. Two kingdoms, two clocks; God writes history on both sides of a divided house."},
{q:"How long did Abijam reign, and who was his mother?", options:["Three years; Maachah the daughter of Abishalom","Forty and one years; Naamah","Two years; Tahpenes","Seventeen years; Zeruah"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:2",verse:"Three years reigned he in Jerusalem. And his mother’s name was Maachah, the daughter of Abishalom.", insight:"Three years, and his mother Maachah. Short reigns still shape nations; use whatever years you are given."},
{q:"How did Abijam walk?", options:["In all the sins of his father; his heart was not perfect with the LORD","Uprightly, as David his father","In the counsel of the prophets","In the ways of Asa"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:3",verse:"And he walked in all the sins of his father, which he had done before him: and his heart was not perfect with the LORD his God, as the heart of David his father.", insight:"He walked in all the sins of his father. Inherited sin is still chosen sin; each generation must break the pattern."},
{q:"Why did the LORD preserve Abijam's line?", options:["For David's sake, to give him a lamp in Jerusalem","Because Abijam repented","For the temple's sake","Because of the priests"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:4",verse:"Nevertheless for David’s sake did the LORD his God give him a lamp in Jerusalem, to set up his son after him, and to establish Jerusalem:", insight:"For David's sake, a lamp in Jerusalem. God kept the light burning for an old promise; His faithfulness outlasts our failures."},
{q:"In what one matter did David turn aside from the LORD's command?", options:["In the matter of Uriah the Hittite","In numbering Israel","In the census of Judah","In the battle of Rabbah"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:5",verse:"Because David did that which was right in the eyes of the LORD, and turned not aside from any thing that he commanded him all the days of his life, save only in the matter of Uriah the Hittite.", insight:"Save only in the matter of Uriah. One exception in a lifetime of obedience; even the best need grace for the worst."},
{q:"In which year of Jeroboam did Asa begin to reign?", options:["The twentieth year","The eighteenth year","The third year","The second year"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:9",verse:"And in the twentieth year of Jeroboam king of Israel reigned Asa over Judah.", insight:"Asa began in Jeroboam's twentieth year. While Israel sank deeper, Judah got a reformer; God raises light in dark times."},
{q:"How long did Asa reign, and who was his mother?", options:["Forty and one years; Maachah the daughter of Abishalom","Three years; Naamah","Twenty and four years; Tahpenes","Two years; Zeruah"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:10",verse:"And forty and one years reigned he in Jerusalem. And his mother’s name was Maachah, the daughter of Abishalom.", insight:"Forty and one years. Long, steady, reforming reigns are built one obedient day at a time."},
{q:"How is Asa's reign summed up?", options:["He did that which was right in the eyes of the LORD, as did David his father","He did evil above all before him","He walked in the sins of Jeroboam","He forsook the LORD in his old age"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:11",verse:"And Asa did that which was right in the eyes of the LORD, as did David his father.", insight:"He did right as David his father. The highest praise in Kings is to be compared to David; aim for that epitaph."},
{q:"What were Asa's first reforms?", options:["He took away the sodomites out of the land, and removed the idols","He built high places","He made golden calves","He taxed the priests"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:12",verse:"And he took away the sodomites out of the land, and removed all the idols that his fathers had made.", insight:"He took away the sodomites and removed the idols. Reform begins with removal; you cannot keep what God says to cast out."},
{q:"What did Asa do to Maachah his mother?", options:["Removed her from being queen, destroyed her idol, and burnt it by the brook Kidron","Crowned her queen over Judah","Sent her away to Egypt","Made her high priestess"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:13",verse:"And also Maachah his mother, even her he removed from being queen, because she had made an idol in a grove; and Asa destroyed her idol, and burnt it by the brook Kidron.", insight:"He removed his own mother from being queen. Righteousness cost him family peace; obey God even when it costs kin."},
{q:"What flaw remained in Asa's reform?", options:["The high places were not removed; nevertheless his heart was perfect","He kept the idols","He restored the sodomites","He forsook the temple"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:14",verse:"But the high places were not removed: nevertheless Asa’s heart was perfect with the LORD all his days.", insight:"The high places were not removed, yet his heart was perfect. Incomplete reform with a whole heart still pleases God; keep pressing on."},
{q:"What did Asa bring into the house of the LORD?", options:["Silver, and gold, and vessels, which his father and he had dedicated","The ark from Shiloh","The brasen shields","Timber from Lebanon"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:15",verse:"And he brought in the things which his father had dedicated, and the things which himself had dedicated, into the house of the LORD, silver, and gold, and vessels.", insight:"He brought dedicated silver and gold into the house. What fathers vowed and sons fulfilled enriched God's house; finish the good your fathers started."},
{q:"What did Baasha build against Judah?", options:["Ramah, that he might not suffer any to go out or come in to Asa","A wall around Tirzah","A fortress at Bethel","A navy at Ezion-geber"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:17",verse:"And Baasha king of Israel went up against Judah, and built Ramah, that he might not suffer any to go out or come in to Asa king of Judah.", insight:"Baasha built Ramah to shut Judah in. Enemies build to contain you; God opens what men close."},
{q:"What did Asa take from the treasures of the house of the LORD?", options:["All the silver and the gold that were left, and sent them to Ben-hadad","The ark of the covenant","The showbread","The altar of brass"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:18",verse:"Then Asa took all the silver and the gold that were left in the treasures of the house of the LORD, and the treasures of the king’s house, and delivered them into the hand of his servants: and king Asa sent them to Ben-hadad, the son of Tabrimon, the son of Hezion, king of Syria, that dwelt at Damascus, saying,", insight:"Asa stripped the temple to pay Syria. Desperation spends sacred things; pray before you raid what belongs to God."},
{q:"What did Asa ask Ben-hadad to do?", options:["Break thy league with Baasha king of Israel","Invade the land of Judah","Give him his daughter","Send him chariots"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:19",verse:"There is a league between me and thee, and between my father and thy father: behold, I have sent unto thee a present of silver and gold; come and break thy league with Baasha king of Israel, that he may depart from me.", insight:"Break thy league with Baasha. Asa bought a foreign sword instead of trusting God's arm; alliances are no substitute for faith."},
{q:"Which cities did Ben-hadad smite?", options:["Ijon, and Dan, and Abel-beth-maachah, and all Cinneroth, with all the land of Naphtali","Jerusalem and Hebron","Bethel and Ai","Gaza and Ashkelon"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:20",verse:"So Ben-hadad hearkened unto king Asa, and sent the captains of the hosts which he had against the cities of Israel, and smote Ijon, and Dan, and Abel-beth-maachah, and all Cinneroth, with all the land of Naphtali.", insight:"Ben-hadad smote the cities of the north. God can turn an enemy's enemy into your deliverer, but the price is always high."},
{q:"What did Asa build with the stones and timber of Ramah?", options:["Geba of Benjamin, and Mizpah","A new palace","A wall around Jerusalem","An altar at Bethel"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:22",verse:"Then king Asa made a proclamation throughout all Judah; none was exempted: and they took away the stones of Ramah, and the timber thereof, wherewith Baasha had builded; and king Asa built with them Geba of Benjamin, and Mizpah.", insight:"With Ramah's stones he built Geba and Mizpah. God recycles the enemy's work into your walls; nothing is wasted."},
{q:"What afflicted Asa in his old age?", options:["He was diseased in his feet","Blindness","Leprosy","A withered hand"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:23",verse:"The rest of all the acts of Asa, and all his might, and all that he did, and the cities which he built, are they not written in the book of the chronicles of the kings of Judah? Nevertheless in the time of his old age he was diseased in his feet.", insight:"In old age he was diseased in his feet. Even reformers grow frail; finish well, limping if you must."},
{q:"Who reigned in Asa's stead?", options:["Jehoshaphat his son","Abijam his brother","Nadab of Israel","Baasha of Israel"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:24",verse:"And Asa slept with his fathers, and was buried with his fathers in the city of David his father: and Jehoshaphat his son reigned in his stead.", insight:"Jehoshaphat his son reigned. A godly father leaves a godly heir; the best legacy is a son who follows."},
{q:"How thoroughly did Baasha destroy the house of Jeroboam?", options:["He left not any that breathed, according to the word of the LORD by Ahijah","He spared the little children","He exiled them to Egypt","He made them his servants"], a:0, ref:"1 Kings 15:29",verse:"And it came to pass, when he reigned, that he smote all the house of Jeroboam; he left not to Jeroboam any that breathed, until he had destroyed him, according unto the saying of the LORD, which he spake by his servant Ahijah the Shilonite:", insight:"He left not any that breathed, as the LORD had spoken by Ahijah. Prophecy fulfilled to the last breath; God's word never fails."}
]}
]);
